{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310552,"nonce":"+w831ndohhjLy1Mg70abIBba+yM=","title":"RESOLVED (710aeba7): closure capture analysis dropped free vars used only inside loops/switch/try → empty make_closure list → SIGSEGV. Fix ...","message":"**RESOLVED 2026-06-18 (commit 710aeba7).** A closure that referenced a variable ONLY inside a loop (or switch/try/etc.) body dropped it from its capture list → at runtime the body read a garbage value for the un-captured var → SIGSEGV. Minimal repro (NO threads):\n```haxe\nvar c = new Box2();\nvar f = () -\u003e { var acc=0; for (i in 0...3) acc += c.bump(); return acc; };  // was exit 139\n```\n\n**ROOT CAUSE:** `compiler/src/ir/tast_to_hir.rs::collect_var_refs_stmt` (used by `compute_captures` to find a lambda's free variables) handled only Expression/VarDeclaration/Return/If/While and then `_ =\u003e {}`-DROPPED For, **ForIn**, Switch, Try, Block, Assignment, Throw, PatternMatch, MacroExpansion. So a var referenced only inside a `for` body was never seen as referenced → `compute_captures` excluded it → `make_closure fn5, []` (EMPTY captures — verified with RAYZOR_DUMP_MIR; the working case where the same var is also touched outside the loop showed `make_closure fn5, [$1]`). The companion `collect_local_defs_stmt` was ALREADY exhaustive (For/ForIn/Block/…); only the var-ref side had the gap, and that asymmetry was the bug.\n\n**FIX:** made `collect_var_refs_stmt` recurse into every sub-statement/expr-bearing variant, and added the expression-form control flow (While/For/ForIn/Conditional/Switch) to `collect_var_refs_expr`. 145-line addition, 1 file.\n\n**IMPACT:** this was the real blocker for ergonomic multi-thread Haxe on top of the new wasm Arc/Mutex ([[perf_wasm_real_arc_mutex]]). A 4-thread `Arc\u003cMutex\u003cCounter\u003e\u003e` loop now runs natively with NO workaround → exactly **400000** (was SIGSEGV). Regression clean: e2e 9/9 + 22/22 + 12/12; haxe 132/133 (only the pre-existing flaky test_exception SIGABRT). The user's \"drop analysis / insertFree pattern\" hint was decisive — same traversal-completeness gap, on the capture side.\n\n**Methodology that cracked it (after a long dead end):** the bisection matrix (/tmp/mt M-series) proved it needed capture + instance-method + loop, and crucially that it was NOT threads (M19 crashes with no Thread.spawn — the MAP_JIT/spawned-thread framing was a RED HERRING the user corrected) and NOT Arc-specific (plain `c.bump()` reproduces). I FIRST mis-fixed it at the env-reload layer in hir_to_mir (stable env-ptr + mutated-captures pre-scan) — it didn't work because the method-call receiver never reaches that use-site; reverted. The decisive evidence was `RAYZOR_DUMP_MIR` showing `make_closure []` vs `[$1]`, which pointed at capture COLLECTION, not the env-reload.\n\n**RESIDUAL (separate, NOT a crash):** mutating a captured value-type in a loop (`n = n+1` inside the closure) returns the unmutated value (n=0) — a by-value-capture store-back limitation (only Unary `++`/`--` store back to the env today, HirStatement::Assign to a captured var does not). Distinct from this capture-analysis bug.\n\n**E0100** (the original ask) is a separate, narrower bug: capturing a raw `@:coreType` (Usize) — `Ptr.fromRaw(addr)` where `addr:Usize` is captured — gives \"[E0100] Captured variable not found in scope\" (the coreType local isn't in symbol_map at capture-collection). Capturing an Arc/heap object does NOT trigger it. Only blocks the raw-`rayzor.Atomic`-over-`Ptr` multi-thread pattern, not the Arc/Mutex one (which now fully works).\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_captured_method_call_in_loop_sigsegv` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}